**Definitions**
- **Compile-time (static) polymorphism:** the method to call is chosen by the **compiler**, from the method name and the argument types. Achieved by **method overloading** (and operator overloading or templates in C++).
- **Runtime (dynamic) polymorphism:** the method is chosen at **runtime**, from the **actual object type**. Achieved by **method overriding** with inheritance.

**Java code showing both**

    class Calculator {
        int add(int a, int b)       { return a + b; }
        double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
    }

    class Animal {
        void sound() { System.out.println("Some sound"); }
    }
    class Dog extends Animal {
        @Override void sound() { System.out.println("Bark"); }
    }

    public class Demo {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            Calculator c = new Calculator();
            System.out.println(c.add(2, 3));     // int version
            System.out.println(c.add(2.5, 3.5)); // double version

            Animal a = new Dog();
            a.sound();                           // prints "Bark"
        }
    }

**Why one is compile time.** `c.add(2, 3)` has int arguments, so the compiler matches the `add(int, int)` signature and binds the call while compiling. Overloads differ only by parameter list, and the return type alone cannot distinguish them.

**Why the other is runtime.** `a` has the reference type `Animal` but the object type `Dog`. The compiler only checks that `Animal` has `sound()`. At runtime the JVM looks at the real object and calls `Dog.sound()` through **dynamic dispatch** (a method table lookup; in C++ this needs the `virtual` keyword and a vtable).

**Points to add**
- Overriding needs the same signature and an inheritance relationship; `static`, `private` and `final` methods are not dynamically dispatched.
- Fields are not polymorphic; they are resolved by reference type.
- Benefit of runtime polymorphism: code written against a base type works with new subclasses.
